From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:28:55 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: drive the Sense halves as one pad, and log for the
 host

With both PSVR2 Sense controllers on at launch, GameController gives
the app one controller, "PlayStation VR2 Sense Controllers (L/R)", and
SDL one pad. A half that connects on its own is a controller of its
own instead: a device run with the controllers joining late logged
only "PlayStation VR2 Sense Controller (R)", and with no left stick the
player could not walk. Hot-plug itself reaches SDL: its MFi observers
run where GameController posts, on the main run loop SwiftUI keeps, so
they need no SDL event pump, and pads were added after the immersive
space opened in earlier runs too.

The pad layer read only the pad that drives, and with two separate
halves the one used last would have taken the drive from the other
rather than adding to it. Now a slot knows from its name whether it
is a left half, a right half, a pair or a whole pad. Open halves are
one pad: the drive goes to the group, buttons are read as down when
either half holds them and each axis as the one further out, so the
left stick, L1, L2 and L3 come from (L) and the right side from (R)
without naming where each half's face buttons land in SDL. A fresh
press on either half is the group's use, so a DualSense or Xbox pad
still takes over on its own fresh press and either half takes the
drive back. A half joining the group that drives releases nothing,
and a half leaving releases nothing either: the merged read in the
same poll lifts what only the gone half held and keeps the rest, so a
trigger held through it is not let go and pressed again.

A pair is a whole pad that replaces its halves. One that arrives
after them closes them and takes the drive straight over with the
pad's state as it was, so what both report held is neither pressed
twice nor dropped; halves are ignored while a pair is open, pairs are
opened first in a poll so halves beside them are never opened only to
be closed, and when the pair goes the halves still connected open
again. Names are read from the joystick, not the gamepad, since SDL
can name a gamepad after a mapping another device shares, and the two
halves differ only in their names.

The log says what arrived: "pad added ... half=left", "pad combined
... with ... as one pad", "pad combined: ... gives way to ...", "pad
half left, ... drives alone", and "pad drives" names every member.
rt_host_log gives the host a line in the same run log, for its own
view of GameController's controllers.

The input self test adds virtual pads named like the halves: L then
R, R then L, a pair after its halves, each half leaving and coming
back, a pair while a DualSense drives, and a DualSense taking over and
handing back, with stick, trigger and button routing checked in each.
